%I A399402 #15 Nov 08 2026 20:44:54
%S A399402 10,16,28,244,512,2048,2188,1594324
%N A399402 Numbers k for which some 1 <= i < j <= k/2 has no prime greater than i dividing both binomial(k,i) and binomial(k,j).
%C A399402 Erdős and Szekeres asked whether for all 1 <= i < j <= k/2 some prime p >= i divides gcd(binomial(k,i), binomial(k,j)) (Erdős Problem 699), and conjectured that p > i holds except in a few special cases. This sequence lists the k where p > i fails. In every known term p = i divides both binomials, so none of these is a counterexample to the question itself.
%C A399402 Erdős and Szekeres gave k = 10, 16, 2048 and (k, i, j) = (28, 5, 14), and explained the i = 2 cases: k = 2^r where 2^r - 1 = p*q with p and q prime. Each known term is k = 2^r with i = 2 or k = 3^m + 1 with i = 3; 28 also fails with (i, j) = (5, 14).
%C A399402 There are no other terms below 10^12 (exhaustive search). 2^41 = 2199023255552 is also a term (i = 2, j = 285920731515, 2^41 - 1 = 13367*164511353), so a(9) <= 2199023255552.
%H A399402 Thomas Bloom, <a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/699">Problem 699</a>, Erdős Problems.
%H A399402 P. Erdős and G. Szekeres, <a href="https://www.renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1978-46.pdf">Some number theoretic problems on binomial coefficients</a>, Austral. Math. Soc. Gaz. 5 (1978), 97-99.
%H A399402 Paul J. Lee, <a href="https://github.com/Paulee314/erdos699">Search code and data for k < 10^12</a>, GitHub, 2026.
%e A399402 10 is a term: gcd(binomial(10,3), binomial(10,5)) = gcd(120, 252) = 12 = 2^2*3, which has no prime factor greater than 3.
%K A399402 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A399402 1,1
%A A399402 _Paul J. Lee_, Nov 01 2026
